 "chunks": [
  {
   "text": "Decades earlier, Leonhard Euler had studied a different sum.",
   "start": 0.75,
   "end": 4.65
  },
  {
   "text": "One, plus a quarter, plus a ninth: one over every square number.",
   "start": 4.65,
   "end": 9.075
  },
  {
   "text": "The total is exactly π²/6.",
   "start": 9.075,
   "end": 12.175
  },
  {
   "text": "With one over n itself, the sum grows forever, though slowly.",
   "start": 12.175,
   "end": 16.025
  },
  {
   "text": "Then Euler found the bridge to the primes.",
   "start": 16.95,
   "end": 19.325
  },
  {
   "text": "For s bigger than one, the sum of 1/nˢ equals a product over the primes.",
   "start": 19.325,
   "end": 24.288
  },
  {
   "text": "Expand each factor as one, plus p to the minus s,",
   "start": 24.288,
   "end": 27.575
  },
  {
   "text": "plus p to the minus two s, and so on, and multiply out:",
   "start": 27.575,
   "end": 31.363
  },
  {
   "text": "since every whole number splits into primes in exactly one way,",
   "start": 31.363,
   "end": 34.875
  },
  {
   "text": "each term appears exactly once.",
   "start": 34.875,
   "end": 37.237
  },
  {
   "text": "That sum is the zeta function.",
   "start": 37.237,
   "end": 39.425
  },
  {
   "text": "One side is about all numbers; the other only about primes.",
   "start": 39.425,
   "end": 43.25
  }
 ],
